<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michelangelo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1475">1475</a>–<a href="/years/1564">1564</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Michelangelo Buonarroti</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michelangelo Buonarroti (1475–1564) was a Florentine sculptor, painter, architect and poet, one of the greatest artists of the Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. His works include the marble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, the ceiling and Last Judgement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and the design of the dome of St Peter's Basilica. He personally chose much of his marble at the quarries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michelangelo.jpg" alt="Michelangelo" width="320" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Daniele da Volterra · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michelangelo_Daniele_da_Volterra_(dettaglio).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…artistic capital of Europe: Sixtus IV built the Sistine Chapel and founded the Vatican Library, Julius II commissioned <b>Michelangelo</b>’s ceiling of the Sistine Chapel and Raphael’s frescoes in the Vatican, and the new basilica of Saint Peter, begun in 1506, was completed by Carlo Maderno and Gian Lorenzo…</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The last republic defended itself during the siege of 1529–1530, with fortifications designed by <b>Michelangelo</b>, until Francesco Ferrucci’s relief army was destroyed at Gavinana.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…of medieval Europe and St Peter’s in Rome; Christian art inspired the icons of Byzantium, the paintings of the Renaissance masters and the sculpture of <b>Michelangelo</b>; and Christian music, from Gregorian chant to the works of Bach and Handel, is at the foundation of the Western musical tradition.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Building took 120 years and drew on <b>Michelangelo</b>, Maderno and Bernini.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-18">18 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After four years of work, <b>Michelangelo</b>'s ceiling frescoes in the Sistine Chapel were unveiled for the feast of All Saints.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-1">1 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its design involved Bramante, <b>Michelangelo</b> and Maderno over 120 years.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-18">18 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Caprese Michelangelo, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.64,11.99&amp;year=1532" aria-label="Caprese Michelangelo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4838 617 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Michelangelo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5288 887h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5288 887h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.64,11.99&amp;year=1532">What was here in 1532? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The best of artists hath no thought to show Which the rough stone in its superfluous shell Doth not include: to break the marble spell Is all the hand that serves the brain can do.&#8221;</p><footer>Sonnet 15 &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1878 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10314/pg10314-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Now hath my life across a stormy sea Like a frail bark reached that wide port where all Are bidden, ere the final reckoning fall Of good and evil for eternity.&#8221;</p><footer>Sonnet 65, to Giorgio Vasari, on the brink of death &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1878 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10314/pg10314-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Sweet is my sleep, but more to be mere stone, So long as ruin and dishonour reign; To bear nought, to feel nought, is my great gain; Then wake me not, speak in an undertone!&#8221;</p><footer>The Speech of Night (epigram on his statue of Night) &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1878 &#183; spoken by his statue of Night, in answer to an admirer's verses &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10314/pg10314-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;From heaven his spirit came, and robed in clay The realms of justice and of mercy trod, Then rose a living man to gaze on God, That he might make the truth as clear as day.&#8221;</p><footer>Sonnet on Dante &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1878 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10314/pg10314-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My beard turns up to heaven; my nape falls in, Fixed on my spine: my breast-bone visibly Grows like a harp: a rich embroidery Bedews my face from brush-drops thick and thin.&#8221;</p><footer>Sonnet to Giovanni da Pistoia, on painting the Sistine Chapel ceiling &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1878 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10314/pg10314-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
